data.content:
The lightest option depends on your target framework:

## .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No new interfaces needed. Accept `TimeProvider` via the constructor:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.IsTrue(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

## Pre-.NET 8 → Minimal `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }

public class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor pattern — default to `SystemClock`, inject a fake in tests.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, `TimeProvider` is the lightest path — zero custom abstractions, first-party fake included. Want me to apply the change to your file?
